/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In 1665 the English scientist Robert Hooke published Micrographia, a book of drawings made through his compound microscope.
   - In the 1670s Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, a cloth merchant in Delft in the Netherlands, made single-lens microscopes that magnified a few hundred times.
   - Lenses improved slowly after that.
   - In 1838 the German botanist Matthias Schleiden concluded that all plant tissues are made of cells.
   - Their theory had a gap.
   - The electron microscope, built by Max Knoll and Ernst Ruska in 1931, revealed the organelles inside cells, and molecular biology showed that the hereditary material is DNA.
   - Examiners often ask for "exceptions to the cell theory".
*/
